//! flags: -p ssa --dump-ssa-preschedule --dump-ssa-postschedule

// `loopify()` as the block graph, which is the thing it actually rewrites.
//
// Everything in tests/bonsai/ssa/ above this directory is a golden of the
// *relooper's* reading of that graph. That is worth pinning too -- it is what
// code generation consumes for a function that is not vectorized -- but it is
// a reconstruction, and a reconstruction is a lossy witness for a rewrite:
// two different graphs can reloop to the same statements, and a `while` in
// the output does not say which edge the transform added or where it went.
// These goldens are the graph.
//
// Read the two dumps against each other. Preschedule, both functions end in a
// call to themselves and a continuation:
//
//     callc countdown(@1, @2) !countdown_call_cont_2()
//     !countdown_call_cont_2(@3 : i32): ret @3
//
// Postschedule, that call is gone. In its place the entry jumps into a loop
// header carrying the parameters, and what was the recursive call is a jump
// back to that header with the arguments it would have passed. The
// continuation block is still printed and has no predecessors: a tail call's
// continuation only returns what the call returned, so once the call is an
// edge nothing reaches it. It is unreachable rather than removed, which is
// visible here and is not visible anywhere else.
//
// The difference between the two functions is which parameters get carried.
// `fill` passes `out` and `i` through unchanged, so they stay block arguments
// of the entry and are not threaded round the loop; `n` and `acc` change every
// time, so they are. Nothing in the relooped statements says which is which as
// plainly as the block arguments do.
func fill(n : i32, acc : i32, out : mut array[i32, 4], i : i32) -> void {
  if (n <= 0) {
    out[i] = acc;
    return;
  }
  fill(n - 1, acc + n, out, i);
  return;
}

func countdown(n : i32, acc : i32) -> i32 {
  if (n <= 0) {
    return acc;
  }
  return countdown(n - 1, acc + n);
}

schedule {
  fill.loopify();
  countdown.loopify();
}
